L000306	\textit{Loss of proximity effect:} For generic, non-resonant values of $\tau$, the splitting probabilities depend smoothly on $\tau$, as shown by the red symbols in Fig.~\ref{letter-fig-resonance}, and satisfy the normalization condition $P_L(x_0)+P_R(x_0)=1$. Their expressions are
L000307	\begin{equation}
L000308	\scalebox{0.95}{$
L000309	\begin{split}
L000310	P_L(x _0) = \frac{1}{2}+ \xi (x _0,N, \tau),~ P_R(x _0) = \frac{1}{2}-\xi (x _0,N, \tau),
L000311	\end{split}$} \label{letter-neq-2}
L000312	\end{equation}
L000313	where the function $ \xi (x _0,N, \tau)$ represents the interference effect between  the single-target detection amplitudes $\chi_{n}^{(\pm)}$ in Eq.~\eqref{letter-neq-1}. It can be computed exactly and is provided in Eq.~\eqref{SI-4} at the End Matter. For example, when $x_0=4$ and $N=5$, we get
L000314	\begin{equation}
L000315	\scalebox{0.85}{$
L000316	\begin{split}
L000317	\xi (4,5, \tau) = -\frac{\sqrt{3} \sin \left(\frac{\tau}{2} \right)^2 \sin \left( \tau \right) \sin \left( \sqrt{3}  \tau \right) }{4+ \cos \left( 2  \tau \right)  + \cos \left(  \tau \right) \left[  -1+ \cos \left( \sqrt{3 } \tau \right) \left\{ -5+  \cos \left(  \tau \right) \right\} \right]}.
L000318	\end{split}$} \label{ajniuyqb}
L000319	\end{equation}
L000320	As shown in Fig.~\ref{letter-fig-resonance}, over a wide range of $\tau$ values, we find $P_L(4) > 1/2$, and hence $P_R(4)<1/2$ according to Eq.~\eqref{letter-neq-2}. Thus, the proximity effect is lost, $P_R(4) < P_L(4)$, even though the walker $(x_0=4)$ is initially closer to the right boundary $(x_R=5)$. This behavior contrasts with the classical case in Eq.~\eqref{lett-unitary-eqn-classical}.
