L000379	\textit{Spectral origin of the transition:} \bluew{Eq.~\eqref{letter-neq-2} shows that deviations of the splitting probabilities from $1/2$ originate from the term $\xi(x_0,N, \tau)$. As mentioned before, this term represents an interference effect between $\chi_n^{(\pm)}$. However, it can nevertheless be expressed in terms of the eigenvalues of either $\mathcal{S}_{-} \equiv (\mathbbm{1}-\ket{d_{-}}\bra{d_{-}})U(\tau)$ or $\mathcal{S}_{+}\equiv (\mathbbm{1}-\ket{d_{+}}\bra{d_{+}})U(\tau)$. Here $\mathcal{S}_{\pm}$ are the survival operators in the auxiliary single-target detection problems in Eq.~\eqref{letter-neq-1}. Below we explain the flat-to-fluctuating transition using $\mathcal{S}_{-}$ and relegate the corresponding discussion using $\mathcal{S}_{+}$ to the SM~\cite{SMSingh2}.}
