L000398	Increasing $\tau$ further, the points $a_1(\tau)$ and $a_2(\tau)$ move past each other, and $\{ \lambda_{-} \}$ are no longer confined to a single arc; see Fig.~\ref{letter-fig-pole}(c). Instead, their arrangement becomes irregular in the complex plane, with eigenvalues spreading nonuniformly inside the unit disk. As a result, the contributions associated with different $\lambda_-$ no longer cancel, and $\xi(x_0,N \gg 1,\tau > \tau_c) \neq 0$. This leads to the departure of the splitting probabilities from their flat value of $1/2$ via Eq.~\eqref{letter-neq-2} for $\tau > \tau_c$. In this way, the transition in the splitting probabilities is directly linked to the qualitative reorganization of the eigenvalues $\{ \lambda_- \}$ as $\tau$ crosses $\tau_c$; see \cite{SMSingh2} for further details.
